<p>Turkey said NATO air and missile defenses in the eastern Mediterranean destroyed a ballistic missile it described as fired from Iran and headed toward Turkish airspace on Wednesday, after it crossed Iraq and Syria. Turkish officials reported no casualties and said debris from the defensive interceptor landed in Hatay province without causing damage. Several outlets described the interception as a notable escalation in the wider Israel–US–Iran conflict, framing it as the first reported instance in the current fighting of a projectile heading toward NATO member territory and warning it could raise the risk of spillover or miscalculation. Ankara urged restraint and said it would keep consulting with NATO and allies, while reiterating that it will defend its territory and airspace and reserving the right to respond to hostile acts.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/oUv6tSTjLMCgPYEubMUNuucz4BqDgmLy6BsoS6k8uX8mjTVuhrH4jgzf-Zc58rNdjt7i0YrUithSYdS6SS-EAiWIjxapmSD2OGsjEpu6MQ1aOVZLqrOqvgHyYjB_AmNqrz4_x3ITKj4SeNocq-yOJAYuVGDc2DsTQFU6emtXWWK2xQ25dC6_kQELABvBTrsZHW7NrAYJ' alt='Iranians crossing into Turkey in Van, Turkey, on Tuesday. Turkey, a NATO member, shares a 300-mile border with Iran.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Where debris fell (and what it was): Turkey’s defense ministry said a fragment that landed in the Dörtyol district of Hatay province came from the air-defense munition used in the interception. It reported no fatalities or injuries.</li><li>Incirlik and airspace policy: The New York Times noted that southern Turkey’s Incirlik air base hosts American forces, and also reported Turkey has said it will not allow its airspace to be used for attacks on Iran.</li><li>Erdoğan’s message: President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan said Turkey would stay “cautious, prudent, and patient” to protect citizens. He called for a ceasefire and dialogue while opposing attacks targeting Iran.</li><li>Conflict warnings: The Financial Times reported US Defense Secretary Pete Hegseth warning of “larger waves” of attacks as the conflict spreads.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Turkish defense ministry: Said NATO defenses destroyed the missile in time, urged all sides not to widen the conflict, and said Turkey would consult with NATO while reserving the right to respond to hostile acts. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/633770-nato-intercepts-iranian-missile-turkey/'>RT</a>)</li><li>President Recep Tayyip Erdoğan: Cast Turkey’s approach as cautious and focused on keeping citizens safe, and called for a ceasefire and dialogue while opposing attacks targeting Iran. (<a href='https://www.politico.eu/article/nato-intercepts-missile-headed-turkey/'>Politico Europe</a>)</li><li>Turkey’s Center for Combating Disinformation: Pushed back on social-media claims that Turkey supported recent attacks on Iran, saying the allegations are unfounded and meant to mislead the public. (<a href='https://www.rt.com/news/633770-nato-intercepts-iranian-missile-turkey/'>RT</a>)</li><li>Al-Monitor analysis: Called the incident a significant escalation, noting Iran had not previously targeted Turkey, a NATO member, during the conflict. (<a href='https://www.al-monitor.com/originals/2026/03/turkey-intercepts-iran-fired-ballistic-munition'>Al-Monitor</a>)</li><li>DW live-blog context: In its coverage of the interception, DW also pointed to broader developments in the war, including uncertainty around Iran’s leadership succession and Israeli threats against any successor to Khamenei. (<a href='https://www.dw.com/en/iran-war-nato-destroys-iranian-missile-headed-toward-turkey/live-76207066'>Deutsche Welle</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul><li><a href='https://www.rt.com/news/633770-nato-intercepts-iranian-missile-turkey/'>NATO intercepts ‘Iranian missile’ near Türkiye</a> - rt.com</li><li><a href='https://www.ft.com/content/9a9b0127-1014-4186-99e3-893603011cef'>Turkey says Nato intercepted Iranian missile heading for its airspace</a> - ft.com</li><li><a href='https://